WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
- We are here with Ben
Affleck, Matt Damon,

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.300
and Chris Tucker, y'all,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.280
give it up!
(audience applauds)

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.750
Together they star in "Air."

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.400
It's in theater starting April 5th.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.660
It's such an excellent film.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.493
So Matt and Ben,

00:00:10.493 --> 00:00:12.180
did you meet Michael
Jordan before doing this?

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.173
Did you feel like-
- Chris knows him.

00:00:14.173 --> 00:00:15.006
- Of course you know him!

00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:15.839
- I know Michael.
(audience laughs)

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:17.310
I know Michael.
- Oh my god!

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.750
- You really do know him? (laughs)

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.420
- Yeah, Michael, I met him years ago.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.070
I used to take my son to Santa Barbara

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.530
to his camp he did for kids every year.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.270
And I really was going
to see Michael myself,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.590
but I used my kid,
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.170
just to get in there and
get my autograph first.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.037
So I said, "Oh, here's my son.

00:00:33.037 --> 00:00:34.260
"You can do his autograph too!"

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.093
But I remember
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:36.300
one time he rode in my car

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.010
and I'll never forget that,

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.240
because we were all going out to dinner

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.500
after the camp and all that stuff.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.007
And I couldn't believe he was in my car.

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:43.350
"Dude, this your car?"

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.183
It was a new Bentley.

00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:45.016
"It's a brand new Bentley?

00:00:45.016 --> 00:00:46.020
"This is nice."

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:46.853
I was looking at him like,

00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:48.151
"I'm never gonna sell this car."

00:00:48.151 --> 00:00:49.312
(audience laughs)

00:00:49.312 --> 00:00:50.760
"Michael Jordan is in my car!"

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.282
- And also like, "Don't crash." (laughs)

00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:54.813
- (laughs) Yeah, like,
"Don't hurt his knees!

00:00:55.927 --> 00:00:56.760
"Everybody'll kill me."

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.983
So, oh man, doing this
movie is just a treat.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.260
- I've heard he's super
competitive, though,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.616
so did you ever play him or anything?

00:01:02.616 --> 00:01:03.449
- Oh, never played.

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:04.680
He won't play me in anything.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.513
Some night I was down

00:01:05.513 --> 00:01:07.243
at his golf course-
- Are you afraid to play him?

00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:08.910
- And his friends was like,
"Would you play Chris?"

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.830
He said, "No, no, no!"

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.750
Because he loved to tease you.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.970
I went to his party one
time, the All-Star Weekend.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:15.803
He was like, "What you doing in here?

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:17.842
"How you get in here, what?"
(audience laughs)

00:01:17.842 --> 00:01:18.675
"I've been invited!"

00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:19.683
"No, you weren't, you weren't invited."

00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:20.516
(audience laughs)

00:01:20.516 --> 00:01:21.843
- He's funny, that's funny.
- I said, "Ain't stuntin you."

00:01:21.843 --> 00:01:22.676
(Ben laughs)

00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:23.509
- That's incredible.

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:25.410
Okay, speaking of competitive,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.150
is it true that you are in a,

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.610
this is just interesting
to me, cuz I'm a wordsmith.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.630
I love words.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.770
You're in a "Wordle" group

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.230
with Chris's "Silver Linings"
co-star Bradley Cooper.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.063
Is this true?

00:01:35.063 --> 00:01:36.480
- No, I'm trying to get into it.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.970
Oh, you're not-
- And I'm being velvet roped

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.470
by the bouncer here.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.140
He's telling me that I
gotta get cooler plus-one

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.936
- I don't know-
- It's not easy.

00:01:44.936 --> 00:01:47.460
- We play, so there's "Octordle,"

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.720
which is the eight words.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.553
And then "Quordle,"

00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:50.550
which is the four words,

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.110
and then Wordle, which is the one.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.150
So we play all three and
you add up your score.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.021
- All right, "Will Hunting,"
just get to the point.

00:01:56.021 --> 00:01:59.040
(Chris, Matt, and Kelly laugh)
(audience laughs)

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.730
- No, it's very, very competitive.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.040
Will Arnette is in it, Bradley Cooper,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.540
Jason Bateman.
- And I'm not, which is fine.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.800
I don't resent.
(audience laughs)

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.260
- No, I advised you,
you're welcome to join.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.900
I just advised you, we call
it the Nerdle League. (laughs)

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.317
I was like, "You can
join the Nerdle League,

00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:15.780
"but you might want to
get your scores down."

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.110
- The truth is, they told
me what their scores were.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.480
I was like, "I can get in."

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.320
Then I did it a couple times.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.617
I was like, "I might need to train."

00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:22.260
(audience laughs)

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.430
- That's hard for me.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.080
I love words and stuff, but it's hard.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.030
Especially with the five,
I'm always like, "What?"

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.290
And I have friends that
are brilliant at it.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.090
I wouldn't be invited either.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.440
I do love that y'all play that though.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.380
So what's the last "Wordle"
word that was controversial

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.430
for you guys?

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.920
What's the last one you were like, "No."

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.460
- I'm not a fan of "Quordle,"
which is the four words.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.500
The people are a little sinister,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.530
because they'll do words like rarer

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.847
and I'm like, "You're gonna
do three Rs in one word?

00:02:48.847 --> 00:02:50.676
"You guys suck."
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:50.676 --> 00:02:51.510
(laughs) You know what I mean?

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.160
'Cause it's whenever you flame out,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.560
when you don't get, you blow out.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.520
We call it a blowout,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.900
and I'm just like, "I
blow out on Quordle."

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.130
And it's always "Quordle" that gets me,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.540
and I'm always angry
when I look at the word

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.920
and I'm like, you know,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.729
and it's like pappy or something-

00:03:05.729 --> 00:03:06.562
- I was doing it

00:03:06.562 --> 00:03:07.440
with my mom the other day-
- Three Ps.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.720
- she really objected to sooth.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:10.950
Sooth was the word.
- Sooth, yeah.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.510
- I said, "No, it's
truth, it's like forsooth.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.477
And she said, "Well, that's archaic.

00:03:13.477 --> 00:03:14.385
"That's not fair."

00:03:14.385 --> 00:03:15.477
She really-
- This is my mother

00:03:15.477 --> 00:03:18.480
playing Scrabble, she will
yell at me and my sister

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.313
and she'll be like,

00:03:19.313 --> 00:03:20.917
"Qi, really, Q I?

00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:22.710
"Okay, we're going with it, okay, cool."

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:23.580
- If you play Scrabble,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:24.600
you're all about the qi.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.472
- Believe me, all the Q I, or, believe me,

00:03:27.472 --> 00:03:28.630
(indistinct)-
- Q A T.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.008
- If you don't have a U,

00:03:30.008 --> 00:03:31.927
you are screwed.
- K A V A.

00:03:31.927 --> 00:03:33.120
- No, I love words-
- Or 'za,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.950
that's the worst, for pizza, 'za.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.690
- I'm telling my, my sister-
- No, no, I don't buy that.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.250
- Oh my God, I hope my sister-
- It's not

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.690
in the Scrabble dictionary.
- She used 'za

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.490
when we were in New York first Christmas

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.580
and my mom lost her (beep).
(Matt laughs)

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.250
She was like, "That's not a word."

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.330
And she's a teacher,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.670
so it was very hard to swallow.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.500
- The whether-or-not-a-thing-is-a-word
dispute

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.090
has caused some serious fractions.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.730
- Because then you look it
up and everything's a word.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.563
- Everything's a word

00:03:57.563 --> 00:03:59.160
if you go to the Scrabble
dictionary on your phone.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.720
- You can't just use
the "Urban Dictionary"

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.273
cuz everything's slang-
- No, no,

00:04:02.273 --> 00:04:03.398
even in the regular one.
- for something.

00:04:03.398 --> 00:04:05.915
- "How does this mean 50,"

00:04:05.915 --> 00:04:06.748
can't even mention-
- My mom will get

00:04:06.748 --> 00:04:08.070
- What it is.
- So upset about it.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:08.903
It's so funny.

00:04:08.903 --> 00:04:10.577
"'Za okay, we're going with 'za."

00:04:10.577 --> 00:04:13.298
- 'Za, then okay, does
anything, any expression,

00:04:13.298 --> 00:04:15.420
(Kelly laughs)
getting into my arguments now.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.054
- I love the way this interview went.

00:04:17.054 --> 00:04:18.420
(all laugh)

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.527
I know my mom and sister
are gonna be like, "What?

00:04:20.527 --> 00:04:23.250
"You had these people here
and you talked about what?"

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.110
What's your greatest, did
y'all play sports growing up?

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.410
What's your greatest
achievement with sports?

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.243
I played sport.

00:04:29.243 --> 00:04:30.330
I liked playing sports.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.100
I was not-
- I got cut from the B team,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.170
(Kelly laughs)
like Michael Jordan.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.003
I got cut,

00:04:35.003 --> 00:04:36.660
but then I started hosting talent shows

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.490
and found my true passion.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.440
- Your calling?
- Stand-up comedy and acting.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.450
So it was meant to be.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.430
- I liked playing, I wasn't great.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.630
I was just good.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.802
- I lettered in theater.

00:04:48.802 --> 00:04:50.820
(audience laughs)
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.237
That's true.
- You sexy thing, you.

00:04:52.237 --> 00:04:54.810
- I know, I lettered in theater, but-

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.747
- I did too, but I don't tell people.

00:04:56.747 --> 00:04:58.500
(Matt laughs)

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.760
I hit a ground rule double

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.940
when I was 12 years old to tie
the game in the championship.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.470
I mean the local championship.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.060
- I actually don't know what that is.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.020
What is that?
- Ground rule double is-

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.250
- It's not a home run,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.600
but it bounces once

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.010
and then goes over the fence.
(audience laughs)

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.593
- So you get second base.
- It's the closest I came

00:05:12.593 --> 00:05:13.920
to a home run, the left center field.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.263
I will never forget it as long as I live.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.517
When you're a kid, it's-
- I love those moments, yeah.

00:05:19.517 --> 00:05:20.820
- It was incredible.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.070
- I got to take batting
practice in Fenway Park,

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.780
and I hit the Green Monster

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.180
off of a pitch that my father threw me.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.960
So that was like-
(audience applauds)

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.360
- Do you know what's Interesting?

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.790
I have played where the scorer is,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.350
I've been in the Green Monster

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.760
and played catch with my brother.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:36.593
- Oh, really?
- We were like,

00:05:36.593 --> 00:05:37.426
"We just gotta do it

00:05:37.426 --> 00:05:38.717
"'cause we just gotta say we've done it."

00:05:38.717 --> 00:05:39.870
- Yeah, absolutely,
- Fenway's fun.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:40.910
- It was so cool.
- The tours are great.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:41.790
- He was all over there,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.590
'cause in the town they, how
long did you shoot there?

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.563
- We had three weeks to shoot a robbery,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:48.870
and it was amazing.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.490
It's got this labyrinthian,

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:51.883
it's old but they fixed it up,

00:05:51.883 --> 00:05:54.780
so there's all these
secret tunnels and stuff

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.070
and it's one of the few last,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.260
or the old parks around.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.500
That was 1908 or 1912, whenever was built.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.020
And it's a beautiful park.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.460
If you're ever in Boston, I have to say,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.293
check it out, it's a lot of fun.

00:06:06.293 --> 00:06:07.920
- I agree, I love Boston.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.010
My first time to experience
sports outside of Texas,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:11.843
cuz I'm Texan,

00:06:11.843 --> 00:06:13.563
and we are very passionate people,

00:06:13.563 --> 00:06:14.610
(Matt laughs)

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:15.930
so are Bostonians.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.520
So I went to a game,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.297
I sang at a World Series and I was like,

00:06:19.297 --> 00:06:20.559
"I love these people!"

00:06:20.559 --> 00:06:22.650
Everybody was losing their mind.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:23.483
it's very close

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:24.690
to that's mentality.
- That's what's going on

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:25.523
in Boston.
- I know, but it's fun!

00:06:25.523 --> 00:06:27.180
- It's basically what-

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.383
- Why be there unless you're
gonna have a good time?

00:06:28.383 --> 00:06:29.730
See, that's my point.
(audience applauds)

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.650
We have to talk about the actual shoe,

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.090
the shoes are amazing.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.310
I've been a fan since I was a kid.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.870
Do you remember the first pair of sneakers

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.190
that you bought that made you feel cool?

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:39.750
'Cause I grew up with no money

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.660
so most of my poor sneakers
had marks on the floor.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:43.890
But the first pair I bought,

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.563
I just felt like, it was
with my first check, too.

00:06:46.563 --> 00:06:47.880
- That you bought on your own?

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.713
- And I bought,

00:06:48.713 --> 00:06:52.110
from my job I bought, and
I felt, I wore them out.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.520
- I had the Jordan 4s.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:54.353
I bought the Jordan 4s.

00:06:54.353 --> 00:06:57.000
I was about 11th grade, '89,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.114
and I had to save money working

00:06:58.114 --> 00:07:01.050
for my daddy's company,
Tucker Cleaning Service.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:01.920
'Cause people have been asking me,

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.037
and I had to go back and find,

00:07:03.037 --> 00:07:03.870
"What was that shoe?"

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:04.950
It was the Jordan 4s.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.270
But it's the energy man.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.440
Clothes make you feel good.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:08.273
The Jordans make you feel

00:07:08.273 --> 00:07:10.834
like you can jump like
Jordan and all that.

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:12.810
And that's what this movie got in.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.160
People buy shoes

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.170
and buy clothes because
it makes 'em feel good.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.730
It's good for your mental state.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:18.630
- I'm glad you just said that,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.250
because that was the most
interesting thing too

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.270
that I found, there were
a lot of interesting,

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:22.800
I'm gonna say that about everything,

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:23.940
but I had no idea

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.320
that the league only allowed you

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.980
to wear a certain type of
shoe for a certain time.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.060
And then it was like
you had to pay a fine.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.050
I didn't know that either.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.840
- It had to be a 51% white.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:34.673
- [Kelly] I know!

00:07:34.673 --> 00:07:35.790
- They did not allow
the shoe to have color.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:37.080
- I had no idea of that.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:38.190
All this information that's in there,

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:39.510
and then that shoe is revealed

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.640
and you're just like, "It is so magic."

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:42.824
This film is so good.

00:07:42.824 --> 00:07:43.657
- Thank you.
(audience applauds)

00:07:43.657 --> 00:07:44.850
It's so good.
- Thank you.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:45.690
- I loved it.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.220
I thought it was so cool.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.840
So one of the main themes

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.871
of the movie is fighting
for your own self worth.

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:52.770
When do you feel

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.380
like you've had to fight
for that in your life?

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.905
- I think the act of being an actor.

00:07:57.905 --> 00:07:59.060
(all laugh)

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.900
It's like a lifelong-

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.010
- Every time I come on this lot,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.448
I just remember holding my sides

00:08:03.448 --> 00:08:05.317
in some audition and, "No,

00:08:05.317 --> 00:08:06.487
"you don't need to do the second scene.

00:08:06.487 --> 00:08:07.367
"We've seen all we need."

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:09.570
- "Good, thanks."

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.439
- There's a lot of that soul.

00:08:11.439 --> 00:08:13.320
It beats you down, it-

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:14.490
- I love that you talk about that though,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.480
because young actors,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.860
the younger actors are probably like,

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.937
they feel defeated and they see
y'all and they're like, "Aw,

00:08:18.937 --> 00:08:20.647
"they made a movie and made it,

00:08:20.647 --> 00:08:22.500
"and everything was so magical."

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.973
And they don't see everything-
- Listen we had to be

00:08:23.973 --> 00:08:26.340
- That happened before.
- Incredibly lucky very young,

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.170
and still there were probably

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:31.050
500 parts we didn't get, easily.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.086
- So many rejections.

00:08:32.086 --> 00:08:33.480
It's a helpful thing

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.070
about having your best friend do it too,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.050
because we used to call
it getting okay thanks'ed.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.273
You'd go into a room and
you'd pour your heart out

00:08:40.273 --> 00:08:42.240
for a casting director and a director

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.920
and they'd go like, "Okay, thanks."

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.810
And you'd realize, "I
didn't get the part."

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:46.920
Meanwhile you've been sitting out there

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.290
for 45 minutes waiting as
the last person was in there

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:50.340
and they come out and they're all laughing

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.420
and hugging and, "Oh, see you later."

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.010
And you're like, "Oh,
well, that went well."

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.360
And then you're out in five minutes

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.193
and you're like,

00:08:55.193 --> 00:08:57.030
and all you really have is we'd go home.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.460
We lived together with his little brother,

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.207
and we'd just go sit
there and, "How'd it go?"

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.727
"I got okay, thanks'ed," and
"Ah, it's all right, man,

00:09:03.727 --> 00:09:04.560
"the hell with that."

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:05.550
You know what I mean,
they don't know what they,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:06.780
and you kind of-
- It's a job interview

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.760
where you're also pouring your heart out

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:09.847
and crying too.

00:09:09.847 --> 00:09:11.317
"Why won't you?"

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:12.817
"Thanks, that's fine."

00:09:12.817 --> 00:09:13.650
"Are we good?

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.000
"Okay, 'cause I'm sobbing."

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.680
I'm remembering the most traumatic moment

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.147
in my life and I can go home.

00:09:18.147 --> 00:09:20.460
- But cool, "Okay, thanks." (laughs)

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.710
What do you feel like for you,

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:23.760
what do you feel like was a moment

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.260
for you when you were younger?

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.600
- Oh man, I would say, stand-up comedy,

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:30.896
going on stages,

00:09:30.896 --> 00:09:32.295
making sure that you're ready

00:09:32.295 --> 00:09:34.080
and you have audience.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.190
Sometimes you have great shows,

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.080
sometimes you have lukewarm shows,

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:37.913
so you gotta go back

00:09:37.913 --> 00:09:38.820
to the clubs-
- Did you ever really struggle

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.010
or were you just like
pop bang right outta-

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:41.887
- Yeah, I didn't really
struggle that much.

00:09:41.887 --> 00:09:42.720
(Matt and Ben laugh)
(audience laughs)

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.659
I was trying to find something.

00:09:43.659 --> 00:09:46.589
- Oh my God, you're like in
here with binoculars searching.

00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:47.935
(Chris laughs)

00:09:47.935 --> 00:09:49.380
- Well that's a blessing that we had.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.550
I had standup to fall on,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:51.900
cuz when it's no work in Hollywood,

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.280
we was on the road every weekend

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.050
and we got that audience response,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.180
so it kept you up, kept you going.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.170
- It is interesting too,

00:09:58.170 --> 00:09:59.910
because the audience plays such a big role

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.070
and I don't know that
everybody knows that.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:03.420
But I think we realized in Covid,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.400
and everybody's doing
drive-in theater shows

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:06.240
and stuff like that where you don't

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.830
really get that interaction.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.810
But that live, or if
you've done live theater,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.280
that live interaction,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.660
you get drunk off that energy.
- And that's like-

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.700
- Even sitting in the room with a movie,

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:16.122
which as a director is the only time

00:10:16.122 --> 00:10:17.220
you get that-
- You feel it differently.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.720
- This movie in particular,

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:19.650
because it's funny

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.310
and it's optimistic and
the acting's so good.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.400
You just like it more with other
people around you laughing.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.020
- I agree too, 'cause I
watched it with friends

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:29.340
and I was like,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.470
just having that you're
almost looking at each other

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.480
like you're rooting for people to win,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.300
and you're rooting for them
to believe in them, and, yeah.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.410
Well, we're almost out of time,

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.610
but I have to ask,

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.567
did you ever get distracted
by Ben's hair in the movie?

00:10:42.567 --> 00:10:43.547
(Matt and Chris laugh)

00:10:43.547 --> 00:10:45.530
'Cause I feel like it should
have gotten, it's ability-

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:46.950
- It I did, but it helped me.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:48.300
It helped me in seeing where we were in.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.133
I was like, "Man,

00:10:49.133 --> 00:10:51.270
"this is something right here!"

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.010
- Is that a real perm?
- If I look crazy,

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:53.843
everyone else will know

00:10:53.843 --> 00:10:55.423
they're gonna look better by comparison.

00:10:55.423 --> 00:10:57.277
- The windsuit with the
glasses and the perm?

00:10:57.277 --> 00:10:58.860
- That was historically documented.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.600
I did not put on one piece of clothing

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.356
that I did not see was worn by-

00:11:02.356 --> 00:11:03.922
- [Kelly] But don't tell me
you weren't excited. (laughs)

00:11:03.922 --> 00:11:04.755
- I loved it

00:11:04.755 --> 00:11:05.700
I still wear the stuff at home.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.403
- Everybody could hear you
coming. (imitates whooshing)

00:11:07.403 --> 00:11:08.657
- He's so good in this movie

00:11:08.657 --> 00:11:10.680
= And a big shout out to Charlese,

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:11.513
our costume designer-
- Did an amazing job.

00:11:11.513 --> 00:11:13.320
- Who crushed it.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:14.494
This is all stuff,

00:11:14.494 --> 00:11:17.940
when Sunny, the real Sunny came to the set

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:18.773
with his wife

00:11:18.773 --> 00:11:19.606
and they looked at me

00:11:19.606 --> 00:11:21.000
and Charlene was standing there

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:22.987
and they turned to her
and and his wife said,

00:11:22.987 --> 00:11:24.432
"Did you raid his closet?"

00:11:24.432 --> 00:11:26.100
I was wearing all this stuff,

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.290
and she had just kind of intuited exactly

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:29.430
what he would wear.
- In fact, I think

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.340
we have a gift for you.
- It's important to-

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.330
- I brought you something.

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:34.990
Somebody's gonna materialize.

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:36.113
There we go.

00:11:36.113 --> 00:11:37.140
Look at this.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:38.520
I thought that this is-

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:39.810
- The only thing is,
you have to wear them.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:40.680
- Ooh, fun!

00:11:41.648 --> 00:11:45.238
(audience applauds)
You bet your ass. (laughs)

00:11:45.238 --> 00:11:46.567
(Matt laughs)

00:11:46.567 --> 00:11:49.600
I wish I was skiing right now. (laughs)

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.327
This is incredible.
- Let's go!

00:11:51.327 --> 00:11:54.901
You're welcome, America.
(audience cheers)

00:11:54.901 --> 00:11:57.869
You're welcome, just a
little of this action, like

00:11:57.869 --> 00:11:59.199
"Ferris Bueller's Day Off."
- Did you just say,

00:11:59.199 --> 00:12:00.037
"You're welcome, America"?

00:12:00.037 --> 00:12:01.140
- "You're welcome, America."

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.100
- That's so good.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.870
- I know you wanna make-
- Come for a minute

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.090
with Daft Punk, we
thought it would be like-

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:06.923
- They're super cool,

00:12:06.923 --> 00:12:08.880
I'm gonna actually go out with it, fun.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:09.810
One more time,

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:13.212
for Ben Affleck, Matt
Damon, and Chris Tucker.

00:12:13.212 --> 00:12:14.407
(audience applauds)
"Air" Is in theaters

00:12:14.407 --> 00:12:15.687
everywhere starting Friday,

00:12:15.687 --> 00:12:18.243
and look for it coming
soon on Prime Video.

